<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Achieng has Ksh <m>{60\,000}</m> to save for <m>{2}</m> years.</p><p>Offer A: a SACCO fixed deposit paying <m>{12}\%</m> per annum simple interest.</p><p>Offer B: a bank account paying <m>{11}\%</m> per annum compound interest, added each year.</p><p>(a) Find the amount after <m>{2}</m> years with offer A.</p><p>Ksh <fillin characters="10" name="ans1"/>  </p><p>(b) Find the amount after <m>{2}</m> years with offer B, to the nearest shilling.</p><p>Ksh <fillin characters="10" name="ans2"/>  </p><p>(c) Which offer gives more? [ Offer A | Offer B ]  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Simple interest: <m>{60\,000} + \dfrac{{60\,000} \times {12} \times {2}}{100} = {60\,000} + {14\,400} = {74\,400}</m>.</p><p>(b) Compound interest: <m>{60\,000} \times {1.11}^{ {2} } = {73\,926}</m>.</p><p>(c) Offer A gives more, by Ksh <m>{474}</m>.</p><p>Simple interest adds the same sum every year; compound interest multiplies by the same number every year. A higher simple rate can win over a few years, but over a longer time compound interest pulls ahead.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>74400</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>73926</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p>Offer A</p></answer>
</stack-static>